Mikan Stomp

Ingredients: J-POP + ELECTRONIC BODY MUSIC (EBM) + VIBRANT, TROPICAL & SUNNY + Cymbals
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from a heat-warped cassette tape discovered inside a 1989 seaside elementary school time capsule.

Mikan Stomp grafts the militaristic, 16th-note bassline sequencing of classic Belgian and German Electronic Body Music directly onto the euphoric, major-seventh chord architecture and group-vocal hooks of late-Showa and Heisei J-pop. Instead of shadowy bunker claustrophobia, the sound explodes into blinding Pacific daylight: rapid-fire analog synthesizer pulses rumble beneath sparkling digital bells, sun-bleached brass, and a relentless storm of high-frequency cymbals—from razor-sharp splash chokes to shimmering, wash-heavy ride cymbals—creating an intoxicating collision of physical sweat and beachside pop exuberance.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 124–132 BPM. A rigid, metronomic 4/4 body-music stomp powered by relentless 16th-note monophonic bass runs, offset by breezy, swung sixteenth-note cymbal subdivisions and buoyant beach-parade syncopations.
- Harmonic Palette: Sparkling Japanese idol progressions (IVmaj7–V7–iii7–vi) built on bright FM synth bells, colliding against pitch-quantized minor-key bass drones and sudden, euphoric whole-tone modulations in the chorus.
- Production Signatures: "Choke-Gating" (hand-hammered splash and china cymbals aggressively gated to rhythmic sidechains, mimicking waves crashing against breakwaters); "Tape-Bake Flutter" (subtle wow, flutter, and cassette tape saturation applied across top-end vocal stacks); and "Industrial Gang-Hooks" (sugary pop vocals recorded in mass unison, delivered with drill-team precision and zero vibrato).
- Dynamics: Abrupt tension shifts where bone-rattling, minimalist bass verses suddenly detonate into panoramic, blindingly bright choruses saturated with wall-of-sound cymbals, air horns, and triumphant analog brass.

Scene & Origin Lore
Mikan Stomp ignited along the tangerine-groved coastlines of Shizuoka and Suruga Bay in the late 2010s, after municipal excavators unearthed a concrete capsule buried in 1989 beneath a condemned oceanside playground. Inside was an unlabeled chrome cassette that had survived decades of sub-surface coastal heat: an unauthorized radio mix where a pirate broadcast of European EBM had magnetically bled into an afternoon idol countdown show, warping into a single, unified frequency. Local beachside warehouse workers and circuit-bending club kids treated the tape like sacred scripture, constructing makeshift PA sound systems among coastal citrus packing sheds to duplicate its bizarre kinetic energy. The look of the scene quickly crystalized: high-visibility dockworker jumpsuits cut off at the knee, fluorescent zinc oxide sunscreen applied like military war paint, heavy jump-boots, and sun-faded hibiscus aloha shirts. Gatherings, known locally as "Sorting Shifts," combine hyper-disciplined synchronized foot-stomping drills with communal crate-stacking dances under the blazing noon sun.

Central Instruments: Roland SH-101 and Korg MS-20 (for overdriven 16th-note bass sequencers), Yamaha DX7 (crystalline bells and FM electric pianos), Paiste 2002 cymbals (20-inch heavy ride bells, unlathed 10-inch splash chokes, and trash china cymbals), Roland TR-909 kick/snare engine, and tropical steel-pan samples.

For Fans Of: DAF (Deutsch Amerikanische Freundschaft), Wink, Yellow Magic Orchestra, Nitzer Ebb

