Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Cast Iron Sweats Citrus
- Story & Context: When town archivist Daiki Numata pried open the corroded 1989 time capsule at Shimizu harbor, the warped cassette inside reeked of sea-salt rust and burnt magnetic glue. He carelessly set the brittle tape beside a sizzling cast-iron skillet of reducing bitter-orange marmalade, where the bubbling citrus steam softened the warped ribbon just enough for a portable deck to finally track its grooves. As the tape head caught, an ungodly fusion of an idol girl-group cadence and a jackhammer sequencer roared across the salt-stained tile floor, prompting Numata to drop his wooden spoon and shout the song's hypnotic loop: "Boil the rind down till the iron yields!" By sunset, bootleg dubs of that four-track recording were blaring from car stereos parked along every pier in the prefecture.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An artistic, hyper-vibrant album art still of a seasoned black cast iron skillet bubbling with electric neon-orange citrus slices and molten cassette tape ribbons on an open beach stove, filmed in warm afternoon sunlight with dramatic salt spray, heat distortion ripples, and bold Japanese typography reading the lyric hook.

Track 2: "Who Salted the Dashi?"
- Story & Context: Keyboardist Chiyo Koga drafted this high-velocity confession inside the sweltering galley of the Shimizu Fish Market cafeteria after a twelve-hour shift scaling bonito. Her knuckles were raw and stung with coarse sea salt as she punched the melody into a battery-powered Roland synth, trying to match the brutal tempo of the cafeteria's industrial vent fans. During the lunch rush, she accidentally dumped half a cup of rock salt into the master stockpot, causing an all-out shouting match with the head chef that ended with Chiyo slamming her ladle onto the counter and screaming into the dining room: "Too much salt makes the pulse keep time!" Instead of rioting, fifty dockworkers began slamming their wooden chopsticks on the aluminum tables in perfect 16th-note sync with her pounding bassline.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic, grainy retro photography inside a humid Japanese fish market kitchen, capturing a stainless steel table strewn with open salt cellars, dried bonito shavings, and an analog synthesizer, with sharp shafts of equatorial sunlight cutting through cooking steam and water splashing on concrete tiles.

Track 3: Zero Azimuth Sukiyaki
- Story & Context: On the final night of the Suruga Bay harvest, hundreds of dancers gathered around a twenty-foot communal sukiyaki pan heated by charcoal braziers along the rocky shoreline. Producer Kenzo Tani brought out the original time capsule cassette, now held together with yellowed electrical tape, and intentionally misaligned the playback deck's azimuth screw to let the high frequencies scream like white-hot grease. The local maritime police arrived to confiscate the generator, but they were held back by an unbroken ring of dancers in rubber fishing boots, kicking up clouds of gray volcanic sand while singing in full unison toward the black ocean: "Feed the iron dish until the sun comes back!" When the tape finally snapped at 4:00 AM, the crowd replaced the missing sequencers by chanting the bassline until dawn broke over the horizon.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A dynamic wide-angle night photograph of an open-air beach gathering around a massive glowing sukiyaki pan on dark volcanic sand, surrounded by dancers in boots and bright aloha shirts, with metallic sparks flying upward into the deep indigo night sky where brass cymbals catch the warm firelight.

